Q: Log2 implementation with cygwin64 seems inefficient A: <blockquote>
<p>Would the use of a divide be specific to cygwin64 or would all versions of gcc use divide instead of multiply?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A <em>usual</em> reason to prefer division over multiplication is that the result <em>tends</em> to be more correct (lower <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_in_the_last_place" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ULP</a>), especially in edge cases.</p>
<p>To test that, the harness below exercises select <code>double</code> values near and at a powers of 2 and some of the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vtSmfws0_To" rel="nofollow noreferrer">usual suspects</a>.  It then compares the results to a <code>long double</code> version.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>
<p>I could see <em>some</em> preference for <code>log2()</code> for values near <em>small</em> powers of 2, yet really nothing to suggest it is worth the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79868900/log2-implementation-with-cygwin64-seems-inefficient#comment140944111_79868900">12%</a> time hit as reported by OP.</p>
<p><strong>Further</strong></p>
<p>More testing could include random tests over billions of <code>double</code> to see what the average and worst case ULP differences are.  Perhaps that will suggest a preference?</p>
<p>(Further testing - not shown - indicated a preference for <code>constant*log()</code>.)</p>
<hr />
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;float.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
#include &lt;math.h&gt;
#define FMT_LA &quot;% -26.16La&quot;
#define FMT_A &quot;% -23.13a&quot;

void testlog2(double x) {
  long double yr = log2l(x);
  double y0 = log2(x);
  double ym = 1.442695040888963387 * log(x);
  long double d0 = y0 - yr;
  long double dm = ym - yr;
  const char *s = 0;
  if (fabsl(d0) &lt; fabsl(dm)) s = &quot;log2() better&quot;;
  if (fabsl(d0) &gt; fabsl(dm)) s = &quot;constant*log() better&quot;;
  if (s) {
    printf(&quot;x:&quot; FMT_A &quot;, log2l():&quot; FMT_LA &quot;, log2():&quot; FMT_A &quot;, 1.442...*log():&quot; FMT_A &quot;, %s\n&quot;, //
      x,yr,y0,ym,s);
  }
}
int main() {
#if 0
  uint32_t i;
  for (i = 1 ; i &lt;= 10 ; i++)
    printf(&quot;log %u: %.20f\n&quot;, i, log2((double)i));
  for (i = 1 ; i &lt;= 10 ; i++)
    printf(&quot;log %u: %.20f\n&quot;, i, 1.442695040888963387 * log((double)i));
#endif
  for (int i = -50; i &lt;= +50; i++) {
    double x = pow(2,i);
    x = nextafter(x, 0);
    for (int step = 0; step &lt; 3; step++) {
      x = nextafter(x, x+x);
      testlog2(x);
    }
  }
  double x[] = { 0, DBL_TRUE_MIN, DBL_MIN, 1, DBL_MAX };
  size_t n = sizeof x/sizeof x[0];
  for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {
    testlog2(nextafter(x[i],0));
    testlog2(x[i]);
    testlog2(nextafter(x[i],x[i]*2));
  }
}
</code></pre>
